  "Question": "If \\(x\\ne0,\\) \\(\\frac{x}{2}=y^2\\) and \\(\\frac{x}{4}=4y,\\) then \\(x\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(8\\)",
    "B": "\\(16\\)",
    "C": "\\(32\\)",
    "D": "\\(64\\)",
    "E": "\\(128\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The second equation gives \\(x=16y\\), while the first gives \\(x=2y^2\\). Equating these expressions eliminates \\(x\\):\n\\[\n2y^2=16y.\n\\]\nSince \\(x=16y\\ne0\\), we know \\(y\\ne0\\). We may therefore divide by \\(2y\\), obtaining \\(y=8\\). Thus\n\\[\nx=16\\cdot8=\\boxed{128},\n\\]\nchoice E. Indeed, \\(128/2=8^2\\) and \\(128/4=4\\cdot8\\), so both original equations hold.",
